  <!-- ============ MAIN CONTENT ============ -->
  <main id="content">

    <section id="home" class="hero">
      <span class="kicker">Module 5 · Train · Day 1</span>
      <h1>Your First Trained Model<span class="sub">An MLP That Reads Handwriting</span></h1>
      <p class="lede">Time to cash in everything. You have a forward pass (M2), a loss and a training loop (M3). Point them at real data — thousands of handwritten digits — and you get a working model that reads them. This is the "hello world" of deep learning, and it's built entirely from pieces you already know.</p>
      <div class="goal"><span class="gic" aria-hidden="true">🎯</span><div><b>By the end, you'll be able to:</b> describe a 2-layer MLP classifier for MNIST (784 → 128 → 10), and explain why we measure accuracy on a separate test set.</div></div>
    </section>

    <!-- ===== 1. WHAT IS IT ===== -->
    <section class="module-section" id="s1" data-sec="what">
  <div class="sec-head"><span class="sec-num s-what">1</span><span class="sec-h">Putting M1–M3 together on real data</span><span class="sec-tag">What is it</span></div>
  <div class="sec-body">
      <div class="callout c-info"><span class="ic">🧭</span><div><b>What this assumes:</b> M1 (arrays/reshape), M2 (a layer = <code>relu(x@W+b)</code>, the forward pass), M3 (cross-entropy loss, the training loop). Today just aims them at data. <b>Nothing new needed.</b></div></div>

      <h4>The task and the data</h4>
      <p><span class="term" data-tip="A classic dataset of 70,000 handwritten digit images (0-9), each 28x28 grayscale pixels. The 'hello world' of deep learning.">MNIST</span> is 70,000 small images of handwritten digits 0–9, each 28×28 pixels. Flatten one image (M1 Day 1) and it's a vector of <strong>784 numbers</strong>. The goal: read the digit.</p>

      <h4>The model</h4>
      <p>A <span class="term" data-tip="Multi-Layer Perceptron: a stack of dense layers with activations. The simplest kind of neural network.">MLP</span> (multi-layer perceptron) — the forward pass from M2: <code>784 → 128 → 10</code>. The last layer gives 10 scores, one per digit; the highest score is the prediction.</p>

      <h4>Train, then test</h4>
      <p>Train with the M3 loop on 60,000 images, then measure <span class="term" data-tip="The fraction of test examples the model classifies correctly. Measured on data not used in training.">accuracy</span> on 10,000 <strong>held-out</strong> images the model never trained on. Testing on unseen data is how you know it truly learned, not just memorized. Keep going 👇</p>
      <div class="callout c-info"><span class="ic">🏭</span><div><b>A real one:</b> the cross-entropy loss you're about to use on 10 digit classes is the exact same formula a GPT-style model uses to predict its next token out of a 100,000+ word vocabulary.</div></div>
      <div class="callout c-warn"><span class="ic">😕</span><div><b>为什么这里容易卡住 · Why this trips people up:</b> 很多人以为"第一个真实模型"一定有全新的东西。In plain terms: there is <em>nothing new</em> here — it is M1's reshape, M2's <code>relu(x@W+b)</code>, and M3's loss + loop, all pointed at real data. The one genuinely new idea is small but important: you grade on a <em>held-out</em> test set, not the data you trained on.</div></div>
      <button class="gotit" type="button">Got the setup — let's go</button>
    </div>
</section>

    <!-- ===== 2. INTUITION ===== -->
    <section class="module-section" id="s2" data-sec="intuition">
  <div class="sec-head"><span class="sec-num s-study">2</span><span class="sec-h">Flashcards and a pop quiz</span><span class="sec-tag">Intuition</span></div>
  <div class="sec-body">
      <div class="relate">
        <div class="card"><span class="big">🃏</span><h5>Training = flashcards</h5><p>Show the model a digit, let it guess, tell it the answer, let it adjust (the M3 loop). Repeat over 60,000 cards until it rarely misses.</p></div>
        <div class="card"><span class="big">📝</span><h5>Testing = a pop quiz</h5><p>Then quiz it on 10,000 <strong>new</strong> digits it never studied. Accuracy on those is the real score — it can't have memorized them.</p></div>
      </div>
      <p><strong>In one line:</strong> practice on the training set with the loop, then grade on a held-out test set to see if it really learned.</p>
      <p><b>直觉 / Intuition:</b> 训练像刷抽认卡，测试像突击小考——只有做没背过的题，分数才算数。 The technical point is that accuracy only means something on data the model never trained on; that held-out score is your first taste of real evaluation.</p>
      <div class="callout c-warn"><span class="ic">⚠️</span><div>Where the analogy breaks: a student can't literally memorize 60,000 cards, but a big model can start to <span class="term" data-tip="When a model fits the training data well but does worse on new data. The gap between train and test accuracy reveals it.">overfit</span> — score high on training yet worse on the test set. Watching the two scores diverge is how you catch it.</div></div>
      <button class="gotit" type="button">Got the picture</button>
    </div>
</section>

    <!-- ===== 3. PLAYGROUND ===== -->
    <section class="module-section" id="s3" data-sec="play">
  <div class="sec-head"><span class="sec-num s-play">3</span><span class="sec-h">Data → model → training</span><span class="sec-tag">Playground</span></div>
  <div class="sec-body">
      <p>Below is a simulated Python terminal. Follow a digit from pixels to prediction, then train. <strong>Click all three</strong> to finish this section.</p>
      <div class="play">
        <div class="play-btns">
          <button class="play-btn" data-demo="data" type="button">① an image = 784 numbers</button>
          <button class="play-btn" data-demo="model" type="button">② the MLP forward pass</button>
          <button class="play-btn" data-demo="train" type="button">③ train → accuracy climbs</button>
        </div>
        <div class="play-console" id="console"><span class="dim"># click a button above to start</span></div>
        <div class="play-take" id="take"><span class="dim">Each click shows a one-line "what this means" here.</span></div>
      </div>
      <p style="font-size:.85rem;color:var(--muted)">Tip: every piece here is from M1–M4 — reshape (M1), <code>relu(x@W+b)</code> (M2), cross-entropy + loop (M3), softmax (M4 D3).</p>
      <button class="gotit" type="button" disabled>try all three first</button>
    </div>
</section>

    <!-- ===== 4. MECHANISM & WHY ===== -->
    <section class="module-section" id="s4" data-sec="why">
  <div class="sec-head"><span class="sec-num s-study">4</span><span class="sec-h">The full model, and why this is the template for everything</span><span class="sec-tag">Mechanism &amp; why</span></div>
  <div class="sec-body">
      <h4>The whole thing, end to end</h4>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Input:</strong> a 28×28 image → <code>reshape</code> to 784 (M1 Day 1).</li>
        <li><strong>Layer 1:</strong> <code>h = relu(x @ W1 + b1)</code>, shapes <code>(784,128)</code> → 128 hidden units (M2).</li>
        <li><strong>Layer 2:</strong> <code>logits = h @ W2 + b2</code>, shapes <code>(128,10)</code> → 10 digit scores (M2).</li>
        <li><strong>Softmax + loss:</strong> softmax the 10 scores into probabilities (M4 D3), compare to the true digit with cross-entropy (M3 D1).</li>
        <li><strong>Train:</strong> backprop + update, looped over the data (M3 D2–3). Then measure accuracy on the held-out test set.</li>
      </ul>

      <div class="callout c-info"><span class="ic">🪜</span><div><b>Math Ladder — the MLP forward pass</b>
<br><b>1 · In words:</b> multiply the pixels by the first weights and bend the result with ReLU to get hidden features, then multiply those by the second weights to get one score per digit.
<br><b>2 · The formula:</b> <code>logits = relu(x·W1+b1)·W2 + b2</code> — <code>x</code> = one flattened image <code>(1,784)</code>; <code>W1 (784,128), b1 (128)</code>; <code>relu</code> zeroes negatives → <code>h (1,128)</code>; <code>W2 (128,10), b2 (10)</code> → <code>logits (1,10)</code>, one score per digit.
<br><b>3 · Tiny numbers:</b> <code>x=[2,-3]</code>, <code>W1=I</code>, <code>b1=0</code> → pre-activation <code>[2,-3]</code> → <code>relu → [2,0]</code> (the −3 is clipped). Then <code>W2=[[1],[1]]</code> → score <code>= 2·1 + 0·1 = 2</code>.
<br><b>4 · Sanity check:</b> <code>h</code> has one entry per hidden unit (128) and <code>logits</code> has one per class (10). ReLU can only zero or keep a value — never make it negative. A negative entry after ReLU means a bug.</div></div>

      <h4>Why a frontier lab cares</h4>
      <p>This tiny classifier is the <strong>same recipe</strong> as a giant LLM: represent input as vectors, forward pass, loss, backprop, loop. In fact the loss function is <em>identical</em> — the cross-entropy loss you used on 10 digit classes is the exact same formula a GPT-style model uses to predict its next token out of a 100,000+ word vocabulary. Only the scale and the architecture change. And the <strong>train/test split + accuracy</strong> is your first taste of evaluation — the discipline that keeps you honest as models get bigger.</p>
      <div class="callout c-info"><span class="ic">🔗</span><div><b>Remember this chain:</b> image → flatten → MLP forward → softmax → cross-entropy loss → train loop → test accuracy. Every module so far (M1–M4) shows up in this one working model.</div></div>

      <h4>The staff lens — one silent failure, one trade-off</h4>
      <p>The test-set number is the whole point of this lesson — so the ways it can lie to you matter just as much as the model itself.</p>
      <div class="callout c-warn"><span class="ic">⚠️</span><div><b>Failure mode (silent): the test set leaks into training.</b> This is a bug that doesn't announce itself. If you scale the pixels using a mean and range computed over <em>all</em> 70,000 images (train + test together), or you shuffle before splitting and the same image lands in both sets, the model has secretly seen the test data. Training runs fine, no error prints, and the test accuracy looks great — but it is inflated, and the model does worse on truly new digits. A senior engineer in <b>code review</b> checks that the split happens <em>first</em> and that every statistic (scaling, normalization) is computed on the training set only, then applied to the test set.</div></div>
      <div class="callout c-info"><span class="ic">⚖️</span><div><b>Trade-off: model size vs. honest generalization.</b> A bigger hidden layer (say 1024 instead of 128) can push training accuracy higher, but it also has more room to memorize the 60,000 training digits instead of learning the shapes. In a <b>design review</b> you weigh this: more capacity can raise the ceiling, but only if you also watch the train-vs-test gap and add regularization. Chasing training accuracy alone buys you a model that shines in practice and fails on real handwriting.</div></div>
      <div class="callout c-ok"><span class="ic">🎤</span><div><b>Say this in an interview:</b> "An MNIST classifier is a 2-layer MLP: flatten the 784 pixels, <code>h = relu(x·W1+b1)</code> gives 128 hidden features, <code>logits = h·W2+b2</code> gives 10 class scores, then softmax + cross-entropy is the loss and you train with the standard backprop loop. It's the same recipe as an LLM — the cross-entropy over 10 classes is identical to next-token loss over a 100k-word vocab. The nuance is evaluation discipline: accuracy only means something on a held-out test set, and any scaling statistic must be computed on train data only, or the test number is silently inflated."</div></div>
      <button class="gotit" type="button">Got the full model</button>
    </div>
</section>

    <!-- ===== 5. BUILD IT UP ===== -->
    <section class="module-section" id="s5" data-sec="build">
  <div class="sec-head"><span class="sec-num s-code">5</span><span class="sec-h">From pixels to a trained classifier — scroll, don't click</span><span class="sec-tag">Build it up</span></div>
  <div class="sec-body">
      <p>Here is the whole model built one step at a time. <strong>Scroll down slowly</strong> — each piece fades in with a short note.</p>
      <div class="build-hint">↓ scroll to reveal each piece ↓</div>
      <div class="build" id="build"></div>
      <button class="gotit" type="button" disabled>scroll through the build first</button>
    </div>
</section>

    <!-- ===== 6. QUIZ ===== -->
    <section class="module-section" id="s6" data-sec="quiz">
  <div class="sec-head"><span class="sec-num s-quiz">6</span><span class="sec-h">Click an answer — instant feedback on each</span><span class="sec-tag">Quiz</span></div>
  <div class="sec-body">
      <p>4 questions, instant feedback. <strong>Answer all four</strong> to complete today.</p>
      <div id="quiz"></div>
      <button class="gotit" type="button" disabled>answer all four first</button>
    </div>
</section>

    <!-- ===== 7. PRODUCE ===== -->
    <section class="module-section" id="s7" data-sec="produce">
  <div class="sec-head"><span class="sec-num s-produce">7</span><span class="sec-h">Leave today's evidence (strongly recommended)</span><span class="sec-tag">Produce</span></div>
  <div class="sec-body">
      <p>Understanding ≠ being able to write it. Train a real classifier yourself — that's what makes the day count. Pick one:</p>
      <h4>Option A · write it yourself</h4>
      <p>Create <code>sessions/m04-first-model-mlp/day-01-mlp-mnist/experiment.py</code>. Load MNIST (e.g. via <code>sklearn.datasets.fetch_openml</code> or keras), flatten to 784, build a 784→128→10 MLP forward pass, train with cross-entropy + gradient descent over a few epochs, and print test accuracy each epoch. Aim for ~95%+. Run with <code>python3 sessions/m04-first-model-mlp/day-01-mlp-mnist/experiment.py</code>.</p>
      <h4>Option B · let Claude build it, then read it</h4>
      <p>Copy the prompt below back into Claude Code. It has Claude Code create the file, write the code, and run it for you.</p>
      <div class="prompt">
        <div class="prompt-h"><span class="prompt-l">ask Claude to build it</span><button class="copy" type="button" data-copy="#pp">📋 copy</button></div>
        <pre class="prompt-t" id="pp">Help me build my Module 5 Day 1 artifact.

Create sessions/m04-first-model-mlp/day-01-mlp-mnist/experiment.py that, with a comment on each step, trains an MLP on MNIST from scratch (NumPy only if feasible, else a tiny framework):
1. Loads MNIST, flattens each 28x28 image to 784, scales to [0,1]; splits into train/test.
2. Builds a 2-layer MLP: 784 -> 128 (ReLU) -> 10; softmax at the output.
3. Trains with cross-entropy loss and gradient descent for a few epochs over mini-batches (reuse the forward/loss/backprop/update loop from M2-M3).
4. Prints training loss and TEST accuracy each epoch; confirms test accuracy climbs to ~95%+.
Then run it and paste the final accuracy at the bottom as a comment.</pre>
      </div>
      <h4>Acceptance criteria</h4>
      <ul>
        <li>The forward pass maps a batch of shape <code>(batch, 784)</code> to <code>(batch, 10)</code>.</li>
        <li>Test accuracy is reported on <em>held-out</em> data (not the training set) and reaches ~95%+.</li>
        <li>Any pixel-scaling statistic is computed on the training set only, then applied to the test set.</li>
      </ul>
      <div class="callout c-info"><span class="ic">📓</span><div><b>5-minute research log · 5 分钟研究笔记:</b> 关页面前，用自己的话写三行 — before you close the tab, write three lines in <code>sessions/m04-first-model-mlp/day-01-mlp-mnist/log.md</code>: (1) your test accuracy and how it compared to the training accuracy; (2) the number or shape that surprised you; (3) one thing you're still unsure about.</div></div>
      <button class="gotit" type="button">Done</button>
    </div>
</section>

    <div class="fin" id="fin" role="status" aria-hidden="true">
      <span class="em" aria-hidden="true">🎉</span>
      <h3>Module 5 · Day 1 complete! 🏆</h3>
      <p>Nice work — you've completed <b>Your First Trained Model</b>.<br>Next up: <b>The Backward Pass, Wired by Hand</b>.</p>
    </div>

